\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {At last, Liese Academy came into full view. Even half-dead from the climb, I had to admit\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it was a sight. The place had stood for centuries, carved out of sturdy limestone, every wall etched with intricate elven designs that still shimmered faintly when the light hit them. That\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s where all magic came from, after all. The elves had been here long before us, leaving behind their art, their spells, and their architecture. And Liese Academy never let anyone forget it.}
\par\plain {The students shuffled across the courtyard in their uniforms\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 maroon cloaks fluttering behind them and the ever-present pointed hats. Those hats. I used to wear one too, back in the day, and I hated every cursed second of it. The straps itched, the brim fell forward if you breathed too hard, and if the wind caught you, well\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 good luck looking dignified as your hat tumbled across the courtyard like a runaway wheel of cheese. To this day, I maintain that the chin straps were designed as a form of psychological torture.}
\par\plain {I slowed my bike to a stop at the gates, trying not to look too much like I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d just fought a losing war with gravity.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oi, I remember you,\u8221\'94 a voice barked. The security guard on duty, Gary, strolled over, grinning like he\u8217\'92d just caught me stealing biscuits. His accent was as thick as ever. \u8220\'93Yer Edward bloody Welton!\u8221\'94 He cackled, pointing at me like I was an old pub joke come to life.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, hey Gary,\u8221\'94 I said, trying to catch my breath without looking like I was trying to catch my breath. \u8220\'93Good to see you. How\u8217\'92ve you been?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s been alright, innit? Like it always do,\u8221\'94 Gary said with a shrug. \u8220\'93\u8217\'92Cept I\u8217\'92ve got less kids sneakin\u8217\'92 out into the forest these days.\u8221\'94 He gave me a pointed look, as if I\u8217\'92d been the only student in history with the brilliant idea to climb over the wall after curfew.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ah, yeah,\u8221\'94 I said, raising my hands in mock surrender. \u8220\'93That may have been\u8230\'85 occasionally me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {He snorted. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Occasionally, my arse. You gave me more exercise than me bloody job description.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What\u8217\'92re ye in this time, then?\u8221\'94 he asked, leaning on his spear like we were just two blokes chatting about the weather.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93My sister got in trouble,\u8221\'94 I said with a sigh. \u8220\'93Gotta get her out. You know, runs in the blood.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Gary laughed, a deep, chesty chuckle. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Better go on quick then, afore she burns the place down, eh?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Honestly, with Torrie, I couldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t even rule that out.}}